watch runs a decoder like 'ley decode', but only shows records that pass a
filter, and can hand a match to a notifier. It is how you wait for one thing
on the air: a county's weather alert, a particular station, traffic from a
place. The decoder is any 'ley decoders' lists; the filter and the notifier
work the same whichever one you name, because they read the record every
decoder produces, not the decoder.

By default watch stays attached and streams the matching records, the way
decode streams every record. --detach leaves the job running in the daemon
after ley exits, so the notifier fires with nothing connected -- which is the
point of a watch: an alert must reach you when you are not looking. --detach
prints the job id and how to stop it ('ley jobs cancel') and exits.

The filter is built from the flags, ANDed together. --where is the general
form and works on any field a decoder emits; --near and --county are shortcuts
for two common cases:

  --where field=value   the general test: any field, by name. Also field!=value
                        (not equal), field~value (contains: a whole word, or
                        words in order, ignoring case, so 'text~net control'
                        finds a speech record that says it), and field>value,
                        >=, <, <= (numeric). Repeat for more tests.
  --near LAT,LON --radius R   records with a position within R of a point
                        (R is 10km, 500m, 5nm, 3mi). Position is a field every
                        decoder that has one reports, so this is not tied to any.
  --county FIPS         shorthand for --where fips~FIPS: the county codes a SAME
                        weather alert carries, as the five-digit FIPS code or the
                        six-digit SAME code. Repeat for more; a record matches
                        if it names any of them. A decoder with no 'fips' field
                        never matches, which is the honest answer.

--notify hands each match to a notifier. Bare --notify is a macOS
notification; --notify=webhook:URL POSTs the record, --notify=shell:CMD runs
a command with the record's JSON on stdin (an explicit target takes the '='
form, so a bare --notify can still mean the macOS one). The daemon runs the
notifier, so it fires whether or not ley is attached.

--channel watches what a channel already open hears, in its mode and
through its squelch, as 'ley decode --channel' does; without it a decoder
with no frequency of its own makes its channel in the band plan's mode.

A speech decoder sends its provider every minute of a channel whose squelch
never closes (its squelch off, a mode with no squelch such as WFM, or a band
whose stations never stop transmitting: FM and AM broadcast, NOAA weather
radio), however few records the filter lets through: watch says so and asks
before it starts. --yes skips the question, and is required when stdin is
not a terminal.

--json prints one DecodeRecord per line (NDJSON): the matches and nothing else.

Usage:
  ley watch <decoder> [flags]

Examples:
  ley watch same --county 06009 --notify        # a weather alert for your county
  ley watch aprs --where device_id=LEYTST-1     # one station
  ley watch aprs --where 'temp_c>25'            # only when it is warm
  ley watch aprs --near 37.76,-122.42 --radius 10km
  ley watch same --county 06009 --notify=shell:'say alert' --detach
  ley watch speech --freq 146.52 --where 'text~net control' --notify --detach

Flags:
      --channel string            watch what a channel hears, in its mode and through its squelch: an id, row number from 'ley state' or frequency, or this for the active one
      --count int                 stop after this many matches, e.g. 5 (default: until Ctrl-C)
      --county stringArray        a county the record must name in its 'fips' field: the five-digit FIPS code (06009) or the six-digit SAME code (006009) (repeatable)
      --detach                    leave the job running after ley exits so the notifier fires unattended ('ley jobs cancel' stops it)
      --device string             which radio: an id (dev_...), id prefix or row number from 'ley devices' (default: the first real radio)
      --freq string               watch somewhere other than the recipe's frequency, e.g. 144.8 (a bare number is MHz)
      --gain string               receiver gain: auto, dB such as 30 for the first stage, or stages such as LNA=0,VGA=20 (ley help gain); set when the job tunes the radio, never on one it shares; default: the decoder's recipe
  -h, --help                      help for watch
      --job                       alias for --detach
      --near string               records from around here: LAT,LON, e.g. 37.76,-122.42 (needs --radius)
      --notify string[="macos"]   hand each match to a notifier: bare (macOS notification), webhook:URL or shell:CMD
      --radius string             how far around --near to look: 10km, 500m, 5nm, 3mi
      --take-over                 watch even when somebody is using the radio; it is theirs again afterwards
      --where stringArray         a field test: field=value, field!=value, field~value, field>value (repeatable)
  -y, --yes                       start without asking when every minute of the audio would leave this Mac

Global Flags:
      --ascii           draw with ASCII only (no box, bar or tree glyphs)
      --color string    colour output: auto, always or never (default "auto")
      --json            machine output: proto3 JSON (NDJSON for streams)
      --socket string   daemon socket path (default: user daemon UDS, $LEYLINE_SOCKET)
